# 2. NON-NEGOTIABLE ARCHITECTURAL PRINCIPLES

The API implementation MUST follow these principles.

1. PostgreSQL remains the authoritative source of persistent application data.
2. Supabase Auth remains the authoritative authentication provider unless an existing approved implementation requires otherwise.
3. The backend remains responsible for authorization and business-critical validation.
4. The frontend must never be trusted to calculate authoritative game scores.
5. The frontend must never be trusted to enforce poll voting rules.
6. Administrative actions must be authorized on the server.
7. RLS must remain enabled and correctly configured on exposed Supabase tables.
8. Realtime is a delivery mechanism, not the authoritative database.
9. Realtime events must not bypass authorization.
10. API responses must not expose unnecessary sensitive information.
11. All user-generated content must be validated server-side.
12. Production database records must be preserved during migrations.
13. All consequential operations must have predictable failure behavior.
14. API changes must account for existing frontend consumers and integrations.
15. The application must not depend on manual page refreshes to receive supported live updates.
16. The implementation must remain a modular monolith unless an approved architectural change is made.
17. The system must not introduce unnecessary infrastructure or dependencies.
18. No endpoint may be declared complete until its authentication, authorization, validation, error handling and tests have been verified.

