# 6. HTTP STATUS CODES

Use appropriate HTTP status codes.

| Status | Meaning | Example |
|---|---|---|
| 200 | Successful request | Resource retrieved or updated |
| 201 | Resource created | Post created |
| 202 | Accepted for asynchronous processing | Only where genuinely applicable |
| 204 | Successful operation with no response body | Resource deleted |
| 400 | Invalid request | Malformed request |
| 401 | Authentication required or invalid | Missing/expired session |
| 403 | Authenticated but unauthorized | Restricted administrative action |
| 404 | Resource not found or not visible | Missing post |
| 409 | Conflict | Duplicate or incompatible operation |
| 413 | Payload too large | Upload or request exceeds approved limit |
| 415 | Unsupported media type | Invalid content type |
| 422 | Valid request structure but business validation failed | Invalid voting selection |
| 429 | Rate limit exceeded | Excessive requests |
| 500 | Internal server error | Unexpected failure |
| 503 | Service temporarily unavailable | Required dependency unavailable |

Do not reveal the existence of private resources through inconsistent error behavior.

For example, a private resource may return a not-found response rather than confirming that it exists to an unauthorized user, depending on the approved visibility policy.

