# 13. REALTIME ARCHITECTURE

## 13.1 Purpose

Realtime functionality enables Kea Parrot to update supported interfaces without requiring users to refresh manually.

Supabase Realtime may be used for:

- Relevant feed changes.
- Reactions.
- Comments.
- Poll results.
- Notifications.
- Approved account-status changes.

The actual list must match implemented backend functionality and the approved product requirements.

## 13.2 Realtime is not the source of truth

PostgreSQL remains authoritative.

Realtime communicates changes that have already been persisted.

The frontend must not treat an event as proof that an operation succeeded unless the event represents a confirmed server-side state change.

## 13.3 Realtime event flow

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    A["User Action"] --> B["Next.js API"]
    B --> C["Authentication and Validation"]
    C --> D["Database Transaction"]
    D --> E{"Transaction Successful?"}
    E -->|No| F["Return Error"]
    E -->|Yes| G["Persisted Database Change"]
    G --> H["Realtime Publication"]
    H --> I["Authorized Subscribers"]
    I --> J["Frontend State Reconciliation"]
```

## 13.4 Realtime subscription principles

Every subscription must:

- Be necessary.
- Have a clearly defined purpose.
- Use the correct user or resource context.
- Respect RLS and channel authorization.
- Clean up when no longer needed.
- Handle disconnections.
- Avoid duplicate subscriptions.
- Avoid unnecessary broad table subscriptions.

Do not subscribe every client to every application table.

## 13.5 Subscription lifecycle

A subscription should follow this lifecycle:

1. Establish the authenticated session.
2. Determine the resource or user context.
3. Create the subscription.
4. Listen only for relevant events.
5. Update or invalidate the appropriate UI state.
6. Handle subscription errors.
7. Reconnect where appropriate.
8. Remove the subscription when the component or context is no longer active.

Subscriptions must not leak across users when authentication changes.

---

# 14. REALTIME CHANNEL DESIGN

The following channel names are proposed logical patterns.

They must be checked against existing channels and authorization policies before use.

| Channel pattern | Purpose | Intended audience |
|---|---|---|
| `user:{userId}:notifications` | User-specific notifications | That user |
| `post:{postId}:comments` | Comments for an accessible post | Authorized viewers |
| `post:{postId}:reactions` | Reaction changes | Authorized viewers |
| `poll:{pollId}:results` | Poll result changes | Authorized viewers |
| `feed:global` | Approved global discovery updates | Eligible subscribers |
| `community:{communityId}` | Approved community updates | Eligible members |
| `user:{userId}:account` | Relevant account-status updates | That user |

These names do not automatically create authorization.

The implementation must define actual Supabase channel authorization, topic policies, or database publication policies based on the selected Realtime mechanism.

## 14.1 User-specific channels

User-specific channels must be isolated.

A user must not subscribe to another user's private notification channel by modifying a client-side identifier.

## 14.2 Resource-specific channels

Post, poll, and community channels must enforce resource visibility.

An inaccessible resource must not become accessible merely because the client knows its ID.

## 14.3 Global channels

Global channels must not expose:

- Private content.
- Anonymous author identities.
- Protected user records.
- Internal moderation data.
- Exact location information.

Use global broadcasts carefully to avoid unnecessary fan-out and privacy leaks.

