# 19. RATE LIMITING AND ABUSE PROTECTION

## 19.1 Purpose

Protect Kea Parrot from abusive requests, automated spam, accidental request storms, and resource exhaustion.

## 19.2 Endpoints requiring special attention

Evaluate rate limiting for:

- Authentication-related operations.
- Post creation.
- Comment creation.
- Reactions.
- Poll voting.
- Game session creation.
- Game answer submission.
- Report submission.
- Location requests.
- Notification operations.
- Administrative actions.

## 19.3 Rate-limit dimensions

Depending on the operation, rate limits may consider:

- Authenticated user.
- IP address.
- Resource identifier.
- Account status.
- Operation type.

Do not rely exclusively on client-side throttling.

## 19.4 Rate-limit responses

Use HTTP 429 with a safe error response.

Where appropriate, include retry guidance.

Do not disclose internal rate-limit configuration or security mechanisms.

## 19.5 Implementation

Inspect existing infrastructure before introducing Redis or another rate-limiting service.

Do not add Redis merely because it is common in high-traffic applications.

Use the simplest reliable implementation that meets measured needs.

