# 21. API SECURITY REQUIREMENTS

## 21.1 Transport security

Production API traffic must use HTTPS.

## 21.2 Input security

- Validate all external input.
- Restrict payload size.
- Prevent SQL injection.
- Prevent unsafe HTML rendering.
- Validate resource ownership.
- Reject unexpected fields where appropriate.

## 21.3 Authentication security

- Verify server-side sessions.
- Never trust user IDs from request bodies.
- Protect privileged operations.
- Handle session expiration.
- Avoid leaking authentication details.

## 21.4 Authorization security

- Enforce permissions server-side.
- Enforce resource ownership.
- Respect account restrictions.
- Apply RLS.
- Protect administrative functions.

## 21.5 Abuse prevention

- Rate limit sensitive endpoints.
- Prevent duplicate operations.
- Validate state transitions.
- Protect against unauthorized enumeration.
- Apply approved reporting and moderation rules.

## 21.6 CORS and CSRF

The implementation agent must inspect the existing Next.js deployment and authentication model.

Configure CORS narrowly where cross-origin API access is required.

For cookie-authenticated state-changing requests, evaluate CSRF protection based on the actual cookie, origin and deployment configuration.

Do not apply arbitrary CORS wildcard settings to solve development issues.

## 21.7 Logging security

Never log:

- Passwords.
- Authentication tokens.
- Service-role keys.
- Sensitive personal data without an approved operational need.
- Private location coordinates.
- Confidential moderation information in public logs.

## 21.8 Security review

Every new endpoint must be reviewed for:

- Broken object-level authorization.
- Broken function-level authorization.
- Excessive data exposure.
- Mass assignment.
- Injection.
- Rate-limit weaknesses.
- Insecure direct object references.
- Privilege escalation.
- Sensitive data leakage.

